WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:53.000
This is 99% Invisible. I'm Roman Mars. In 2010, a history of the world in 100 Objects hosted by Neil McGregor premiered on BBC Radio 4 to great acclaim. It was a groundbreaking series that told a complex and interconnected history of the world through 100 objects housed in the British Museum collection. For 2026, BBC Studios decided they wanted to revamp and reimagine that ambitious series, this time focusing on the history of the history of the U.S. United States, coinciding with the 250th anniversary of the signing of the Declaration of Independence. To the great credit of the BBC, they thought that maybe British broadcasters weren't the best equipped to take on the curation of 100 objects that told the history of the United States. So that's when they reached out to me, lifelong American, Roman Mars.
这里是《99%隐形》。我是罗曼·马尔斯。2010年，由尼尔·麦格雷戈主持的《大英博物馆世界简史》在BBC广播四台首播，并大获成功。这是一个开创性的系列节目，通过大英博物馆馆藏的100件物品，讲述了一段复杂且相互关联的世界历史。对于2026年，BBC工作室决定重塑并重新构想这一宏大的系列，这次将重点放在美国历史的历程上，恰逢《独立宣言》签署250周年。BBC非常明智地认为，英国广播公司可能并不是策划这100件讲述美国历史物品的最佳人选。因此，他们找到了我——土生土长的美国人，罗曼·马尔斯。

2
00:00:53.000 --> 00:01:41.000
With our 100 Object series, we knew we wanted to get out of the museum and focus on objects that were overlooked, thrown away, mass produced and misunderstood, not priceless museum pieces, but the everyday things that provide a portal into stories about what America means to itself and to the world at large. But from the beginning, I knew I needed sage advice to do the job right. And so I sought the council of the original 100 Objects host, Neil McGregor. Here's our conversation. What I was hoping you could do to begin with is taking back to January 2010, a history of the world in 100 objects is launching. It starts with a mummy that you saw as a boy. Were you aware
在我们的“100件物品”系列中，我们知道我们想走出博物馆，专注于那些被忽视、被丢弃、大规模生产和被误解的物品，而不是那些无价的博物馆藏品，而是那些为我们提供了一扇窗口，让我们了解美国对自己以及对整个世界意味着什么的日常事物。但从一开始，我就知道我需要明智的建议才能做好这项工作。因此，我寻求了最初的“100件物品”主持人尼尔·麦格雷戈的建议。这是我们的对话。我希望你首先能带我们回到2010年1月，《大英博物馆世界简史》即将推出的时候。它始于你儿时见过的一具木乃伊。你当时意识到

3
00:01:41.000 --> 00:02:22.000
of the phenomenon that this was going to be when you started? No, absolutely not. Absolutely not. I was intimidated. We were all intimidated by the fact that the BBC had committed to a series of 100 programs. We had no idea how it was going to be received at all. And in a strange way, the project it began really almost as an internal project in the British Museum. What's interested me having come from the National Gallery, so a paintings collection, to a museum, the ambition of which was to show really the history of humanity through things, so not an art museum.
这将会成为一种现象吗？不，绝对没有。绝对没有。我当时很紧张。我们都被BBC承诺制作100集节目的事实吓到了。我们根本不知道它会受到怎样的评价。奇怪的是，这个项目最初几乎是大英博物馆内部的一个项目。我从国家美术馆（一个绘画收藏馆）来到这里，对一个旨在通过实物而非艺术品来展示人类历史的博物馆的雄心壮志感到非常感兴趣。

4
00:02:22.000 --> 00:02:47.000
But to try to explore what it meant to put objects all over the world into one building. So it really began as a way of exploring what happens when you think of a museum as a place where different objects are exploring the same kind of human experience. And of all the things in the building, how did you choose the hundred?
但试图探索将世界各地的物品放在同一个建筑里意味着什么。所以它最初确实是一种探索，当你把博物馆看作是一个不同物品探索同一种人类经验的地方时，会发生什么。在馆内所有的物品中，你是如何选择这100件的？

5
00:02:47.000 --> 00:03:12.000
Well, this was the greatest fun. The British Museum is created in the middle of the 18th century. Really, at that moment of the Enlightenment, when people begin to have a totally different notion of what humanity means, it's the physical expression of that enlightenment notion that there is one humanity. So what we decided to do was to try to organise the 100 objects.
嗯，这是最有趣的部分。大英博物馆创建于18世纪中叶。确实，在那个启蒙运动的时刻，当人们开始对人类的意义有了完全不同的概念时，它是那种“人类是一个整体”的启蒙思想的物理表达。所以我们决定尝试组织这100件物品。

6
00:03:12.000 --> 00:03:42.000
Well, the BBC decided that there would be five programmes a week and there needed to be a theme for each week. And we wanted to go effectively from the beginning, the earliest hand tools made by anything that can roughly be called human right up to today. So that gave us the format. And that in each week we would go round the world. So you would take, let's say, you know, 1,000 BCE and look at a theme, let's say, power.
嗯，BBC决定每周播出五集，每一周都需要一个主题。我们想有效地从最初开始，从任何可以大致称为人类的生物制造的最早的手工工具一直讲到今天。这就是我们的格式。而且每周我们都会环游世界。所以，假设我们选取公元前1000年，然后看一个主题，比如“权力”。

7
00:03:42.000 --> 00:04:22.000
And how do different societies represent power at 1,000 BCE? Yeah. But what it meant internally was that for every week, I would sit down with a curator from the Chinese collection, the Indian collection, Egyptian collection, the America's collection, and we would have to think of what objects might speak to each other. Of course, for academics and a particular discipline, this is a totally new way of thinking. There was no connection between ancient Egypt and ancient China. So no Egyptologist was likely to have asked what in my collection might relate to something
不同的社会在公元前1000年是如何表现权力的？是的。但在内部，这意味着每周我都要和中国馆、印度馆、埃及馆、美洲馆的策展人坐下来，我们必须思考哪些物品可以相互对话。当然，对于特定学科的学者来说，这是一种全新的思维方式。古埃及和古中国之间没有联系。所以没有埃及学家会问，我的收藏中有什么可能与

8
00:04:22.000 --> 00:04:40.000
that's happening at the same time in China. Yeah. Because there's no connection. It's an anthropological question asked to, people who are historical scholars. And they found that completely thrilling because it's allowed them all really to look at familiar objects and ask a totally new kind of question
当时中国发生的事情有关。是的。因为没有联系。这是一个向历史学者提出的社会学问题。他们觉得这非常令人兴奋，因为它确实让他们所有人都能审视熟悉的物品，并提出一种全新的问题，

9
00:04:40.000 --> 00:05:08.000
and suggest the commonalities of human behavior wherever we are. We all behave roughly, equally badly, equally well. And surprisingly, more or less in the same way. Yeah. Were there heated arguments? Was there, you know, table pounding when people were deciding which artifacts to... Curators don't normally pound the table.
并暗示无论我们在哪里，人类行为的共同点。我们所有人的表现大致上一样糟糕，也一样好。而且令人惊讶的是，或多或少以同样的方式。是的。有激烈的争论吗？当人们决定选择哪些文物时，有没有拍桌子？策展人通常不会拍桌子。

10
00:05:08.000 --> 00:05:43.000
There was a certain amount of shrill advocacy. Okay. For my particular piece and a certain amount of sulking that others didn't make it. But it was normally extremely good timid. And because it was a new kind of conversation for all of them, very, very excited. And because what we were looking at was not the history of the world, but our history, a very peculiar one. And one of the histories we wanted to tell almost unspoken through it, because every object came from the museum,
确实存在一定程度的尖锐辩护。好吧。为了我特定的藏品，也有一定程度的生闷气，因为其他的没入选。但通常情况非常好。而且因为这对他们所有人来说都是一种新的对话，所以非常非常兴奋。而且因为我们所看的不是世界历史，而是我们的历史，一段非常独特的历史。我们想通过它讲述的几乎未言明的历史之一，因为每一件物品都来自博物馆，

11
00:05:43.000 --> 00:06:12.000
was how did it happen that every one of these objects ultimately wound up in London? Was there a sense that you had to present a variety of objects? Because you could certainly do, you could do a history of the world. in a hundred coins. The joy of a collection at the British Museum is that you have so many different media in which humans articulate what matters to them, which is what all these things are about.
就是这些物品中的每一件最终是如何流落到伦敦的？你是否觉得必须呈现各种各样的物品？因为你当然可以做，你可以用100枚硬币来讲述世界历史。大英博物馆收藏的乐趣在于，人类有如此多不同的媒介来表达对他们重要的事情，而这正是所有这些东西的意义所在。

12
00:06:12.000 --> 00:06:41.000
But the main purpose of doing history through objects was we wanted to allow people who don't have a voice to be heard. History is on the whole, used to mean, of course, just written history. That limits you immediately to that small bit of humanity that wrote. And it also limits you normally to the people that were in control. Because they were the ones that wrote the history. And they made sure it survived.
但通过物品进行历史研究的主要目的是，我们想让那些没有声音的人被听到。历史总的来说，过去当然意味着仅仅是书面历史。这立即将你限制在那一小部分会写作的人类身上。而且它通常也将你限制在掌权的人身上。因为他们是书写历史的人。而且他们确保了历史得以留存。

13
00:06:41.000 --> 00:07:22.000
So part of our project was to have a history told by the people that had never been able to tell their story. By the makers. Through the things they're made. And that, I think, is really important. How do you give a voice to the voiceless of the past? Yeah, yeah. And particularly when you're talking about indigenous histories, then the objects become of huge importance as carrying the stories that weren't written and may not have survived in song or myth. So around the same time in 2010, I launched my show called 99% Invisible, and it's about architecture and design.
所以我们项目的一部分是让那些从未能讲述自己故事的人来讲述历史。通过制造者。通过他们制造的东西。我认为这真的很重要。你如何给过去那些无声的人以声音？是的，是的。特别是当你谈论原住民历史时，这些物品就变得极其重要，因为它们承载了那些没有被记录下来，可能也没有在歌曲或神话中流传下来的故事。所以大约在2010年同一时间，我推出了我的节目《99%隐形》，它是关于建筑和设计的。

14
00:07:22.000 --> 00:07:53.000
and there was a certain kind of perversity to doing a radio show about architecture and design because there's hugely visual things. And you made the same choice to do a radio series on these things that most people take in with their eyes. Why did you do that? What did you get out of that? Well, at one level, it is totally counterintuitive, isn't it? We're talking about things, the materiality of things, how they're made, the skill with which they made, and yet you can't see it.
做一个关于建筑和设计的广播节目有一种反常感，因为这些是极其视觉化的东西。而你做出了同样的选择，对这些大多数人通过眼睛感知的东西制作了一个广播系列。你为什么要这样做？你从中得到了什么？嗯，在某种层面上，这完全是违反直觉的，不是吗？我们谈论的是事物，事物的物质性，它们是如何制造的，制造它们的技巧，但你却看不见它。

15
00:07:53.000 --> 00:08:24.000
This is where I think the BBC radio, they know how to do this. But it's not just that. BBC has, I think, understood very profoundly and built on the fact that if you can only hear something, then you have to imagine what it is. And you can take possession of something you imagine much more fully than something you just see reproduced in front of you. is why I think we're nearly always disappointed when we see a film of a book
我认为这就是BBC广播知道如何做到的地方。但这不仅仅是这样。我认为BBC非常深刻地理解并建立在这样一个事实上：如果你只能听到某样东西，那么你就必须想象它是什么。而且你对想象出来的东西的占有感，远比仅仅看到它在你面前复制出来要完整得多。这就是为什么我认为当我们看一部书的电影时，我们几乎总是感到失望，

16
00:08:24.000 --> 00:09:09.000
because in reading the book we've imagined the carriage of the place and they've become ours, part of the way we live and think. And the same with objects. If you have to do the mental imaginative work of creating the object for yourself, you are invested in it, you possess it in a way that is not really possible, I think, with just an image. I find that if you lead with the story of an object, you fall in love with it in a different way, whereas like your visual reaction to maybe seeing Rosetta Stone is like, I was expecting something more impressive or something like this. But you can tell the sort of the import of it, what it meant, the story of how it was created and, you know, even the mundane way which it existed.
因为在阅读这本书时，我们已经想象了那个地方的马车，它们已经成为了我们的一部分，成为了我们生活和思考方式的一部分。物品也是如此。如果你必须为自己做心理想象工作来创造这个物体，你就投入了其中，你以一种我认为仅仅通过图像无法实现的方式拥有了它。我发现如果你以一个物品的故事开头，你会以一种不同的方式爱上它，而你对看到罗塞塔石碑的视觉反应可能是：我期待着更令人印象深刻的东西，或者类似的东西。但你可以讲述它的重要性，它的意义，它被创造的故事，甚至它存在的那种平凡方式。

17
00:09:09.000 --> 00:09:28.000
I think that's right. Because what we were really trying to tell was 100 biographies of an object. And because we only have one life. but objects have many, many lives. And what we got very interested in was the changing role of the object through time. In ways I don't know, nobody,
我想那是对的。因为我们真正试图讲述的是100个物品的传记。而且因为我们只有一次生命，但物品却有许多许多生命。我们非常感兴趣的是物品随时间推移而改变的角色。以我不知道的方式，没有人，

18
00:09:28.000 --> 00:09:44.000
when the people carve the Rosetta Stone, this is a tax decree, it's a bit of tax law. And it's so boring. Beyond boring. Nobody carving that tax law in the three languages for administrative purposes in the Union
当人们雕刻罗塞塔石碑时，这是一个税收法令，它是一点税法。而且它太无聊了。无聊透顶。没有人为了行政目的用三种语言雕刻那条税法，

19
00:09:44.000 --> 00:10:06.000
had any idea what it would one day come to mean. And there is something wonderful about that in an object, that the object can play a role that nobody could have guessed. Yeah, yeah. It makes their biographies much more engaged, much more alive, I think, than our own dull little predictable lives. Totally.
曾想到它有一天会意味着什么。物品中确实有一些美妙的东西，即物品可以扮演一个没有人能猜到的角色。是的，是的。我认为这让它们的传记比我们自己沉闷、微小、可预测的生活更投入，更生动。完全正确。

20
00:10:06.000 --> 00:10:31.000
So when you wrote and conceived of the series and produced it, you know, you're the head director of this august institution, the British Museum, as people hold it in great esteem. And I wondered if you noticed your relationship with the public changed when you weren't director, Neil McGregor. You were my pal, Neil McGregor, who I'm invited into my home to tell me a story about a cool thing.
所以当你撰写、构思并制作这个系列时，你是大英博物馆这个受人尊敬的机构的馆长。我想知道，当你不再是馆长尼尔·麦格雷戈时，你是否注意到你与公众的关系发生了变化。你成了我的伙伴尼尔·麦格雷戈，我邀请你到我家给我讲一个关于酷东西的故事。

21
00:10:31.000 --> 00:11:08.000
I think everybody's discovered that if you speak a lot on the radio, people feel they know you. And you must have been the same thing, that very disconcerting experience of actually being recognized in a lift by your voice. Yeah, yeah. The first time it happens, it's very, very peculiar. And one of the things that I think is interesting about it is that it allowed us to reaffirm that the public collections in Britain as in the United States are not the collections of the state, but the collections of the people.
我想每个人都发现，如果你在广播里说得很多，人们会觉得他们认识你。你一定也有同样的经历，那种在电梯里因为声音而被认出来的非常令人不安的经历。是的，是的。第一次发生这种情况时，非常非常奇特。我认为关于它有趣的一点是，它让我们重申，英国和美国的公共收藏不是国家的收藏，而是人民的收藏。

22
00:11:08.000 --> 00:11:43.000
I mean, when the British Museum is created in the 7050s, the idea is that it should be the private collection of every citizen. not the collection of the king open to everybody. Completely different conception from the continental European model. So the idea of speaking directly into people's houses about their collection, their things. I mean, everything I was talking about belonged to the people listening to it. Why did you choose to examine the hundred objects chronologically? And what problems did that cause or what solutions did it provide? I think it has to be chronological.
我的意思是，当大英博物馆在1750年代创建时，其理念是它应该是每个公民的私人收藏，而不是向所有人开放的国王的收藏。这与欧洲大陆的模式完全不同。所以，直接向人们的家中讲述他们的收藏、他们的东西的想法。我的意思是，我谈论的一切都属于听它的人。你为什么选择按时间顺序检查这100件物品？这造成了什么问题，或者它提供了什么解决方案？我认为它必须是按时间顺序的。

23
00:11:43.000 --> 00:12:20.000
because one of the things that happens across human issues is that we create a technology and then it helps us do something and then we become completely dependent on it. We can't live without it. The hand axe, the simple stone scraping hand axe is what lets us as humans live anywhere in the world. But then once you're there, you can't live without it. We've seen the same with mobile phones than the last 20 years. It opens something and then it creates what can be a very dangerous dependency. So that was one strand.
因为在人类问题中发生的一件事是，我们创造了一种技术，然后它帮助我们做某事，然后我们变得完全依赖它。没有它我们就无法生活。手斧，简单的石刮手斧，是让我们作为人类生活在世界任何地方的东西。但一旦你到了那里，你就离不开它了。在过去的20年里，我们在手机上也看到了同样的情况。它开启了一些东西，然后它创造了一种可能非常危险的依赖。所以这是一条线索。

24
00:12:20.000 --> 00:12:50.000
And one of the key meta-nourages, I think, of any museum is the growing connectedness of the world. The fact that you can't tell a single story to use Chimamanda Adich's phrase. The story of the world is an interconnected story. So if you like, why we want to do it chronologically was to show that. constantly intensifying power of exchange curiosity about
我认为任何博物馆的关键元叙事之一是世界日益增长的联系。正如奇马曼达·阿迪奇所说，你不能只讲一个故事。世界的故事是一个相互关联的故事。所以如果你愿意，我们想按时间顺序做是为了展示这一点。不断强化的交流好奇心，

25
00:12:50.000 --> 00:13:01.000
other places trade, greed, war, whatever but that has intensified steadily across the millennia until you can't really tell a single
其他地方的贸易、贪婪、战争等等，但这在几千年来稳步加强，直到你真的无法讲述一个单一的

26
00:13:01.000 --> 00:13:09.000
narrative of a national story. And that was one of the points we wanted to make. The idea of a national history, I'm sorry to say
国家故事的叙事。这就是我们想表达的观点之一。国家历史的概念，我很抱歉地说，

27
00:13:09.000 --> 00:13:20.000
that somebody was writing history in the United States, but the idea of and national history is unthinkable without situating that nation. Absolutely. In the global context.
有人在美国写历史，但国家历史的概念在不将该国家置于全球背景下的情况下是不可想象的。绝对是。

28
00:13:20.000 --> 00:13:49.000
We are in agreement, for sure. But it does present this problem is like the beginning set of objects are all objects that are prehistoric. There's not written records about them. Like you have to sort of conceive of the story differently because there isn't a record of how it was made or why it was made. And so your set of stories really gets sort of limited. And then it opens up as you go along. I would disagree. I disagree. It narrows down the more we know about the... I guess that's true.
我们当然同意。但这确实提出了一个问题，即开始的一组物品都是史前物品。没有关于它们的书面记录。就像你必须以不同的方式构思故事，因为没有关于它是如何制造或为什么制造的记录。所以你的故事集确实变得有限了。然后随着你的深入，它就打开了。我不同意。我不同意。我们了解得越多，它就越狭窄……我想那是真的。

29
00:13:49.000 --> 00:14:17.000
I mean, the whole point of, seems to be the central point of any kind of historical investigation is your poetic involvement with somebody else. That you have to think, what is it like to be somebody else? Because that's the basis of all tolerance. I mean, if you're like, the whole venture was really Gulliver's travels. You go off around the world. You find these astonishing things. things, wild things. But the point of Gulliver's travels is not
我的意思是，任何历史调查的核心似乎都是你对他人的诗意参与。你必须思考，成为别人是什么感觉？因为那是所有宽容的基础。我的意思是，如果可以的话，整个冒险实际上是《格列佛游记》。你环游世界。你发现了这些令人惊奇的东西，狂野的东西。但《格列佛游记》的重点不是

30
